LOCATION:Fine Arts Building\, 1201 East 7th Street\, Bloomington\, IN 47405
GEO:39.169084;-86.518997
SUMMARY:Kristýna Milde | Forest as Home: Landscape\, Memory\, Connection
  | Lecture
DESCRIPTION:In this talk\, Kristýna Milde shares their 15-year investiga
 tion into how trees\, forests\, newspapers\, architecture\, urban infrast
 ructure\, and living materials can become sites for questioning the bound
 aries between nature and culture and the search for connection.&#160\;  T
 he Czech-American interdisciplinary artist duo based in Brooklyn\, New Yo
 rk works across sculpture\, site-specific installation\, photography\, an
 d animation\, combining living plants\, transformed materials\, and every
 day objects to create evolving environments that reveal the interconnecte
 dness of forests\, cities\, and the cultural spaces we inhabit.  Recipien
 ts of the 2026 NYSCA/NYFA Artist Fellowship in Sculpture\, their work has
  been featured in The New York Times\, The Brooklyn Rail\, Flash Art\
 , Hyperallergic\, Forbes\, and Czech television and radio\, among oth
 ers. The College of Arts and Sciences and the Eskenazi School of Art\, 
 Architecture + Design gratefully acknowledge Dr. Meredith McKinney (B.A. 
 '65) and Elsa Luise Barthel McKinney (B.A. '65) for their love of the art
 s and their generous support in endowing the McKinney Visiting Artist Ser
 ies at Indiana University Bloomington.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p class="p1">  In this talk\, Kristýna Mil
 de shares their 15-year investigation into how trees\, forests\, newspape
 rs\, architecture\, urban infrastructure\, and living materials can becom
 e sites for questioning the boundaries between nature and culture and the
  search for connection.&#160\;</p><p class="p1">  The Czech-American inte
 rdisciplinary artist duo based in Brooklyn\, New York works across sculpt
 ure\, site-specific installation\, photography\, and animation\, combinin
 g living plants\, transformed materials\, and everyday objects to create 
 evolving environments that reveal the interconnectedness of forests\, cit
 ies\, and the cultural spaces we inhabit.</p><p class="p1">  Recipients o
 f the 2026 NYSCA/NYFA Artist Fellowship in Sculpture\, their work has bee
 n featured in The New York Times\, The Brooklyn Rail\, Flash Art\, 
 Hyperallergic\, Forbes\, and Czech television and radio\, among others.
  </p><p class="p1"><em>The College of Arts and Sciences and the Eskenaz
 i School of Art\, Architecture + Design gratefully acknowledge Dr. Meredi
 th McKinney (B.A. '65) and Elsa Luise Barthel McKinney (B.A. '65) for the
 ir love of the arts and their generous support in endowing the McKinney V
 isiting Artist Series at Indiana University Bloomington.</em></p>
