BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Eskenazi School of Art, Architecture + Design
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261206
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261207
LOCATION:The Republic Building
GEO:39.199929;-85.921713
SUMMARY:Frances Lightbound: The Land Dissolves in Water | Exhibition
DESCRIPTION:Lecture: October 27\, 6:30–8pmExhibition: October 27–Dece
 mber 6\, 2026\, Mon.–Thur. 10am–4pm when IU classes are in session  L
 ightbound studies the production of space in urban environments\, weaving
  narratives across photography\, print\, spatial interventions\, and scul
 pture\, with a focus on materials and sites in transition.  Architecture 
 becomes an entry-point to questions of land use\, ownership and access\, 
 bodily experience and perception\, and timeframes ranging from the human 
 to the geological. Lightbound invites viewers to consider and participate
  in the continual (re)construction of meaning in urban environments.  Fra
 nces Lightbound holds a B.A. from Glasgow School of Art\, Scotland\, and 
 an M.F.A. from the School of the Art Institute of Chicago. Recent solo ex
 hibitions include Louise Hopkins Center for the Arts (Lubbock\, TX)\, RUL
 E Gallery (Marfa\, TX)\, and the John David Mooney Foundation (Chicago\, 
 IL)\; her work has been exhibited internationally at venues in London\, G
 lasgow and Berlin. Lightbound is a Lecturer at School of the Art Institut
 e of Chicago in the departments of Print media and Photography.The J. Irw
 in Miller Architecture Program Lecture and Exhibition Series brings promi
 nent working artists and architects to Columbus. Lectures take place in t
 he auditorium and exhibitions in the gallery\, both located within The Re
 public Building\, 333 Second Street in Columbus. The gallery is open Mond
 ay through Friday from 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. All events are free and open to t
 he public.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p class="p1"><strong>Lecture:</strong> Octo
 ber 27\, 6:30–8pm</p><p class="p1"><strong>Exhibition:</strong> October
  27–December 6\, 2026\, Mon.–Thur. 10am–4pm when IU classes are in 
 session</p><p class="p1">  Lightbound studies the production of space in 
 urban environments\, weaving narratives across photography\, print\, spat
 ial interventions\, and sculpture\, with a focus on materials and sites i
 n transition.</p><p class="p1">  Architecture becomes an entry-point to q
 uestions of land use\, ownership and access\, bodily experience and perce
 ption\, and timeframes ranging from the human to the geological. Lightbou
 nd invites viewers to consider and participate in the continual (re)const
 ruction of meaning in urban environments.</p><p class="p1">  Frances Ligh
 tbound holds a B.A. from Glasgow School of Art\, Scotland\, and an M.F.A.
  from the School of the Art Institute of Chicago. Recent solo exhibitions
  include Louise Hopkins Center for the Arts (Lubbock\, TX)\, RULE Gallery
  (Marfa\, TX)\, and the John David Mooney Foundation (Chicago\, IL)\; her
  work has been exhibited internationally at venues in London\, Glasgow an
 d Berlin. Lightbound is a Lecturer at School of the Art Institute of Chic
 ago in the departments of Print media and Photography.</p><p class="p1"><
 em>The J. Irwin Miller Architecture Program Lecture and Exhibition Series
  brings prominent working artists and architects to Columbus. Lectures ta
 ke place in the auditorium and exhibitions in the gallery\, both located 
 within The Republic Building\, 333 Second Street in Columbus. The gallery
  is open Monday through Friday from 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. All events are free 
 and open to the public.</em></p>
