X-SOURCE:IU Eskenazi School of Art, Architecture + Design
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20270115
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270116
LOCATION:Fine Arts Building\, 1201 East 7th Street\, Bloomington\, IN 47405
GEO:39.169084;-86.518997
SUMMARY:Mathematics as a Medium | Exhibition
DESCRIPTION:Exhibition Opening Reception: January 15\, 6:30–8:30 pmExhi
 bition Dates: January 15–March 6Symposium Dates: January 15–16  Curat
 ed by Associate Professor of Interior Design in the Eskenazi School of Ar
 t\, Architecture + Design Jiangmei Wu\, the exhibition "Mathematics as a 
 Medium" opening Friday\, January 15 at Indiana University's Grunwald Gall
 ery of Art complements a symposium January 15 and 16 convening many of th
 e exhibitors with counterparts from across the international mathematical
  art community. Over three keynotes\, thematic panels\, and workshops\, p
 articipating artists\, designers\, mathematicians\, and scholars will exp
 lore mathematics as an active medium of creative practice and create a sh
 ared space for exchange among disciplines.  The coordinated events seek t
 o usher in a broader understanding of the relationship between mathematic
 s and art. Proceeding from the proposition that mathematics is a medium�
 �such as paint\, stone\, or sound—through which artists and designers c
 reate and cultures express themselves\, the exhibition and symposium are 
 designed more broadly to redefine mathematics as a living\, generative pr
 actice that shapes how we think\, make\, and experience the world.Visit h
 ere for more information about the exhibition. (https://eskenazi.indiana.
 edu/exhibitions/grunwald-gallery/upcoming/2027-01-15-math-medium.html)Vis
 it here for the symposium schedule. (https://eskenazi.indiana.edu/events/
 special-events/upcoming/2027-01-15-math-medium-symposium.html)This projec
 t was supported by Indiana University's Presidential Arts and Humanities 
 Program\; the Office for Research Development\, Indiana University Resear
 ch\; and also at Indiana University Bloomington: Eskenazi School of Art\,
  Architecture + Design\; Department of Mathematics\; and Luddy School of 
 Informatics\, Computing and Engineering Artificial Intelligence Center (L
 AIC).
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p class="p1"><strong>Exhibition Opening Rec
 eption:</strong> January 15\, 6:30–8:30 pm<br /><strong>Exhibition Date
 s</strong>: January 15–March 6<br /><strong>Symposium Dates:</strong> J
 anuary 15–16</p><p class="p1">  Curated by Associate Professor of Inter
 ior Design in the Eskenazi School of Art\, Architecture + Design Jiangmei
  Wu\, the exhibition "Mathematics as a Medium" opening Friday\, January 1
 5 at Indiana University's Grunwald Gallery of Art complements a symposium
  January 15 and 16 convening many of the exhibitors with counterparts fro
 m across the international mathematical art community. Over three keynote
 s\, thematic panels\, and workshops\, participating artists\, designers\,
  mathematicians\, and scholars will explore mathematics as an active medi
 um of creative practice and create a shared space for exchange among disc
 iplines.</p><p class="p1">  The coordinated events seek to usher in a bro
 ader understanding of the relationship between mathematics and art. Proce
 eding from the proposition that mathematics is a medium—such as paint\,
  stone\, or sound—through which artists and designers create and cultur
 es express themselves\, the exhibition and symposium are designed more br
 oadly to redefine mathematics as a living\, generative practice that shap
 es how we think\, make\, and experience the world.</p><p><a href="https:/
 /eskenazi.indiana.edu/exhibitions/grunwald-gallery/upcoming/2027-01-15-ma
 th-medium.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visit here for more inform
 ation about the exhibition.</a></p><p class="p1"><a href="https://eskenaz
 i.indiana.edu/events/special-events/upcoming/2027-01-15-math-medium-sympo
 sium.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visit here for the symposium sc
 hedule.</a></p><p class="p1"><em>This project was supported by Indiana Un
 iversity's Presidential Arts and Humanities Program\; the Office for Rese
 arch Development\, Indiana University Research\; and also at Indiana Univ
 ersity Bloomington: Eskenazi School of Art\, Architecture + Design\; Depa
 rtment of Mathematics\; and Luddy School of Informatics\, Computing and E
 ngineering Artificial Intelligence Center (LAIC).</em></p>
